izes that in the short  term it's impossible to achieve victory  in the war  thus it's seeking to turn the conflict  into a prolonged armed confrontation  aimed at exhausting Ukraine and our  partners  there are now concerns that any new  Russian assault could come from  neighboring Belarus whose Armed Forces  continue to hold combat Readiness drills  close to its border with Ukraine  our correspondent Nick Connelly is in  Kiev he told us why Russia's claims that  it's only hitting military Targets in  Ukraine just don't add up  well obviously they are still claiming  that this is about basically depriving  Ukraine's military of the power and the  logistics to make their successes on the  battlefield happen obviously that  doesn't really cut a lot of ice when  you're here on the ground and seeing the  kind of targets that are being hit but I  think there's a level to which they've  basically given up trying to create uh  kind of coherent narrative for people  here in Ukraine people in the west they  basically know that that audience is  lost to them so this is all about the  story to People Back Home in Russia who  maybe want to believe what they're told  and to people in third countries who  maybe don't have an ability to really  fact check those claims and I think  there's also just a conviction in Moscow  that they are just so big and they are  so important because of their nuclear  status their you know seat at the  security Council that basically people  will have to talk to them whatever they  do whatever the kind of war crimes  potentially committed by their troops  here in Ukraine at the end of the day  outside Powers the US Europe will come  and speak to them so there's basically  not much of a kind of perceived  impulsive kind of you know need to  really stick to the ordinary rules of  War they can basically get away with  that they seems to be what they think  well joining us now for more on the  military situation in Ukraine is Mike  Martin he's a war studies senior fellow  at King's College London good to have  you with us Mike now Ukrainian officials  say they expect Russia to launch another  large-scale offensive early next year  possibly aiming to take the capital Kiev  do you see that happening  I don't actually see that as very likely  um firstly I don't think the Russians  have that intent they've very clearly  signaled that they're interested in the  donbass in the east of the country I  think their designs on Kiva over and  secondly I don't think they have the  capability Russia at the moment is very  much on the defensive in Ukraine and to  launch a huge another offensive all the  way around coming from Belarus I think  is unrealistic  now it's been reported that the U.S is  preparing to send Patriot missile  systems to Ukraine do you think that  would be a game changer  the Patriot missile system is a highly  effective anti-aircraft system and so  what it would do is enable the  ukrainians to at the moment they're sort  of shooting down about 70 of the  missiles that are coming their way they  could increase that coverage and knock  out almost all of the missiles that are  heading towards Ukrainian cities is it  going to change the Strategic balance of  the war no because the Russian attacks  on the Ukrainian cities themselves are  not changing the Strategic balance of  the war but it will mean that a lot more  Ukrainian civilians survive the winter  what about battle tanks Mike there's uh  Ukraine is begging for battle tanks  Germany is under massive pressure to  send them why is nobody sending battle  tanks to Ukraine  they it's a very good question  um so Ukraine needs these tanks in order  to go on the offensive at a large scale  um they are somewhat complex and have  quite long logistical Tales so it's not  just a question of the tanks you need  the crews and the logistics and the fuel  to run them  um but I wonder whether these fall in  the bucket of is this escalatory if we  include these in the p